A||||8||2birds||Poly bushing question for Ogre, any other opinions welcome.||||1||
Z||000000||2birds||01-20-2001||10:44 PM||||Unless I misunderstand it, in your cave article on poly control arm bushings you stated that the bushings should not rotate in the cup but should rotate around the inner metal sleeve.  Any particular reason why it should not rotate against either surface? <BR>I can understand the smaller diameter equals less linear travel part, which sounds like a good thing, but what would be wrong with both inner and outer surfaces moving?||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||01-21-2001||12:09 PM||||The outside of the bushing should not be rotating in the shell. If there is enough clearance to alow this then the shell has been damaged.<P>Also, the shell is not a bearing surface. I.E. it's rough. You don't want the bushing moving against a rough surface or it will squeak and wear. This is why I also made comments that you might want to consider thin end washers if the frame is really rough. <P>The inner sleeve is the only part that should be able to move in the bushing. The sleeve is intended as a bearign surface and is hard and smooth.||||reg||1
Z||000002||2birds||01-21-2001||01:32 PM||||Thanks for the info.  <BR>I want to install grease fittings in mine, and that will make it easier to do.  I tried epoxying nyloc nuts to the outer shell, to give the fitting something to thread into without affecting the bushing's ability to rotate in the shell, but the nuts kept popping off when I put the grease gun on.<BR>  I'm going to drill through to the inner sleeve and groove the inner surface of the bushing. I'll just thread the fitting into the shell and the bushing.  I did this for my anti-sway bar bushings, and it seemed to cut down on the squeaking and harshness a little. <BR>I just haven't got the motivation to pull it apart yet.  It's either too nice a day to tear it apart or it's too cold.||||reg||6
Z||000003||Tom Philips||01-22-2001||08:01 AM||||I drilled the center of all the mounting bolts for the grease fitting then i used a number 50 drill bit and drilled a hole on an angle so that the hole came out about 1/2 inch below the head of the bolt. I then drilled 6 small holes in the poly steel sleeve deburred and cleaned installed in vehicle and put the grease gun to it and no squeaks Just my .02||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||01-22-2001||06:55 PM||||Tom, You also weakened the bolts. likely allot. I would not be at all suprised if you break one.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cooter||01-22-2001||07:47 PM||||And with what theogre said, I have been in a car when the suspension pivit bolt breaks. It aint no fun!||||reg||1
Z||000006||2birds||01-22-2001||09:29 PM||||I think it was common to see grease fittings in hollow shaft shackle bolts for old leaf spring suspensions, but those were much bigger bolts.  <BR>Two of my rear control arm pivot bolts were really pitted when I tore it apart years ago, and the dealer sent me to a fastener company to get them.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tom Philips||01-23-2001||07:26 AM||||Theogre<BR>A #50 drill bit is real small i was able to torque to normal specs and going on a year with out any problems....(i Used new bolts) <BR>Tom ||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||01-23-2001||08:59 PM||||It's still not something I'd recomend doing on such a critical bolt. The problem isn't the size of the holes as much as the creation of an abnormal stress line. The bolt took full torque but the question becomes how is the altered stress patern going to effect it later. It could hold forever or quit tomorow. There's no real way to predict it without running several lab tests. It's much safer to install the grease fitting to work thru the outer shell and bushing. The odds of that method causing a stress crack is low. even if a crack forms the failure can't cause a wreck.||||reg||1
